WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:06.590
Hello.

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.130
Yep.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:08.330
Let's get started.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:11.582
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:16.960
Yeah.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.320
So we are for the
next three lectures

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:23.170
entering the next
step in our journey

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:29.650
about lossless compression
going beyond iid data.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:32.180
OK.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:33.780
So we started the course.

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.080
We talked about very
simple prefix codes,

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:41.220
we talked about entropy,
and then we looked at AEP.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.750
We'll just provide a theoretical
foundation for compression.

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:48.070
And now after that,
the last three or four

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.570
lectures we have been talking
about specific entropy coders.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:53.510
So let's take a
quick tour of those.

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:54.890
We talked about Huffman coding.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:56.150
It's very fast.

00:00:56.150 --> 00:00:58.850
It is the optimal coder when
you are doing a single symbol

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.950
or you are doing a
particular block.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:04.099
And it will achieve entropy when
you work with larger blocks.

00:01:04.099 --> 00:01:07.130
Of course, the complexity
increases exponentially.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:10.067
So it's great in many
settings, but you

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:11.900
run into this complexity
limit when you want

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.610
to really get close to entropy.

00:01:13.610 --> 00:01:17.790
So that was the
first coder we saw.

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:20.370
Actually, any questions about
the project before we move?

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:24.060
Yep.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:25.570
OK.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.240
Then we saw arithmetic coding.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:29.980
This was a different
style of algorithm

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.110
where the entire input
was a single block.

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.050
And we saw that it will
achieve entropy efficiently.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:39.240
Basically, linear time
instead of exponential time

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.680
as in the case of encoding.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:42.510
And it reads the entire
input as a block,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:47.490
getting a very small 2/n
overhead, over entropy.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:49.895
So it's great.

00:01:49.895 --> 00:01:52.020
One main issue is that it
involves certain division

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:52.710
operations.

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.792
When you actually do arithmetic
coding if you do it in a float

00:01:55.792 --> 00:01:57.750
domain, you need floating
point multiplications

00:01:57.750 --> 00:01:58.740
which are expensive.

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.170
If you do it as an integer
which is the more common way,

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:03.690
you need division operations
which are again expensive.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:07.980
So it is good, but you
won't see arithmetic coding

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.930
being used in fast compressors.

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:13.500
It's still used in a lot of
heavy-duty type compressors

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.200
and we'll look at some examples.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.300
One nice property of
arithmetic coding which

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.460
will be important to us
today and next lecture

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.820
is that it works with
very complex probability

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:26.340
models far beyond
anything we have

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:27.690
seen until now in this class.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.480
And we'll see some examples
in the next lecture.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:31.980
OK.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.710
Finally, we looked at
ANS in the last lecture.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.080
There are two variants--
rANS and tANS.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:39.150
We mostly talked about
rANS, but both of them

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:41.070
have their own strengths.

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.870
Again, they achieve
entropy efficiently

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:45.310
by treating the entire
input as a block.

00:02:45.310 --> 00:02:48.405
tANS doesn't quite achieve
entropy, but it gets close.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.790
There is a small
overhead over arithmetic,

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:57.695
so it's not the best
compression, but close enough.

00:02:57.695 --> 00:02:59.320
Both of them are
faster than arithmetic

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.630
coding with tANS being
particularly fast.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.230
It almost approaches
Huffman coding speeds.

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.390
And finally, the
algorithm we see in class

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:08.830
is always very simplistic.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:10.630
It's like a Python pseudocode.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:12.370
People have figured
out how to implement

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.930
tANS in extremely efficient ways
on modern CPU architectures.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:19.288
And that is the reason
why it's becoming

00:03:19.288 --> 00:03:20.455
more and more popular today.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.760
So there is only so much
we can cover in class,

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:34.430
but here are some
links if you are

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:39.680
more interested in these topics
about these entropy coders.

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:44.180
The paper by Jarek
Duda on rANS and tANS.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:47.060
A couple of few blog posts
by people who have actually

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.130
implemented these algorithms.

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:54.050
And really a lot of the
good stuff in compression

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.420
is on this Russian
forum for some reason.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.330
But all the big people like
Yann Collet, the creator

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:03.050
of Zstandard and Jarek Duda,
the creator of ANS, all of them

00:04:03.050 --> 00:04:06.680
are on that forum, and they
comment on all the latest

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:08.090
developments on compression.

00:04:08.090 --> 00:04:09.530
So if you are interested.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:13.240
OK.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:15.910
And then this is a table we
have been seeing a few times

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.320
in the last few lectures.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:22.710
So Huffman coding, 1950s then
1970s, and then very recent,

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:23.740
ANS.

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.540
And they have different
speed profiles,

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:28.330
different compression,
ratios, and so on.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:32.710
So Huffman coding is the
fastest, but worst compression.

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:34.660
Arithmetic coding is
the slow slower one

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.860
but the best compression
basically for the complexity.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.430
And rANS and tANS are
faster than arithmetic

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:44.650
coding and better
than Huffman coding.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.870
So somewhere in the middle.

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:51.340
One thing you note here is that
rANS has a very fast decoding

00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.470
compared to arithmetic coding.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.780
And we'll look at
that in a minute.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:58.090
In many applications
decoding time is crucial

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.005
and rANS wins out on that quite
a bit, and tANS even more.

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.100
OK.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:09.475
So last point on this is that
all of these entropy coders

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.670
are used in practice sometimes
because they are the best

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:16.550
tool for the job and
many times because people

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:19.700
have been using it and it's hard
to switch compression formats,

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:22.400
so just like a C reasons.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.290
But they have their
unique properties

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:26.330
and we will keep
coming back to these

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:29.120
both in the next few lectures
and in lossy compression.

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:32.390
OK.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:36.420
Any questions on this whirlwind
tour of entropy coders?

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:42.190
The main thing we are doing
with this entropy coder

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:45.250
is start with a known
probability distribution.

00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:47.650
You have data drawn according
to that iid probability

00:05:47.650 --> 00:05:51.790
distribution and you want to
compress it to its entropy.

00:05:51.790 --> 00:05:55.030
Basically, that's the block
diagram version of this.

00:05:58.410 --> 00:06:07.100
So you have known P, which is
iid, and you have x1 to xn.

00:06:07.100 --> 00:06:14.730
And you take your entropy
coder and you get bits.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.910
And you want this
number of bits to be

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.415
roughly equal to n
times the entropy of x.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.140
All of these entropy coders
fit this basic block diagram.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:30.630
OK.

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:32.970
Moving on to the quiz
which hopefully we

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:39.585
can do very quickly because
I wrote down the answers.

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:46.410
So the first two
questions were about rANS

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:48.060
encoding and decoding.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:51.060
You just need to follow the
formulas as you saw in class.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:56.320
So we have some encoding step,
we have some decoding step.

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:59.300
A quick thing I want to note
is that see, in the encoding,

00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.760
you need to divide by the
frequency of the symbol.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.870
And divisions are bad.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.680
Basically, that's good to know.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.830
Whereas during encoding, you
see there is only one division

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.770
step, but it's division by M,
which is usually a power of 2.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:17.060
So division by power of 2 is
very, very cheap on a computer

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:21.350
because you just shift left by
or shift right by something.

00:07:21.350 --> 00:07:24.920
So that is the main reason why
the decoding is so much faster.

00:07:24.920 --> 00:07:27.920
If we saw in a couple of slides
ago the decoding for rANS

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.110
is almost double the
speed of the encoding

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:33.020
because the decoding doesn't
have this division operation

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:36.450
that the encoding has.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:37.800
So we have this formula.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.560
Let's just do it.

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:43.860
I don't want to
spend too much time.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.150
Talk to us later if
you didn't get this.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.830
So you are given some alphabet--

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:56.087
0, 1, 2, and you
have your symbols

00:07:56.087 --> 00:07:57.170
with these probabilities--

00:07:57.170 --> 00:07:59.870
3/8, 3/8, 2/8.

00:07:59.870 --> 00:08:01.303
So first thing you
would do is you

00:08:01.303 --> 00:08:03.470
will say that the common
denominator of all of these

00:08:03.470 --> 00:08:06.290
is 8, so you will set
this capital M equal to 8.

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:10.160
And then you will make this
table, which is symbols.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.230
They are frequencies--
so 3, 3, 2,

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:16.520
and the cumulative which is
the running sum of these guys.

00:08:16.520 --> 00:08:18.950
And then you would
just apply the formula.

00:08:18.950 --> 00:08:21.080
So let me do one of these.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.420
So initially the
state is 0, so you

00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:31.130
do like 0 divided by frequency
of 2, which is 2 multiply by 8

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:37.820
plus the cumulative of 2,
which is 6 plus 0 modulo

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:40.925
frequency of 2, which is 6.

00:08:44.159 --> 00:08:47.220
Anybody had any issues
with this question?

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:51.540
Let me know, otherwise, I will
just write the answers for now.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.680
You're just supposed
to follow this formula,

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:55.320
just do the computations.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:02.230
Let me do the next one.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:07.360
So 6 divided by frequency
of 0 is 3 multiplied

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.270
by 8 again plus
cumulative of 0, which

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:20.230
is 0 plus 6 modulo, the
frequency of 0, so 3.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:22.180
So 6 modulo 3 is 0.

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:25.540
So only the first term
survives and you get 16.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:31.780
After you do all of
it you should get 114.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:33.580
Just substitute.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:36.850
The second one we
asked you to decode.

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:40.420
So you were given something
where the final state was 117

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.420
and you had four symbols and you
are supposed to now decode it.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:47.955
Again, make the table.

00:09:52.920 --> 00:09:55.980
Make the table, and
what do we have here?

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:01.680
We will follow the
steps which you can see.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:02.220
Yes.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.760
So let's do this.

00:10:03.760 --> 00:10:04.710
So block_id.

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:08.850
Block_id is 117 divided
by M, which is 8,

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:13.680
and you take the floor,
which if you do it is 14.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:15.600
So block_id will be 14.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:19.410
Slot is 117 modulo 8.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:24.450
So 112 is a multiple of
8, so this will be 5.

00:10:24.450 --> 00:10:28.050
Then this step-- find_bin
cumul_array, slot.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:32.640
So what this means is
you make this thing

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:36.150
where you mark the
different symbols.

00:10:36.150 --> 00:10:40.440
So the frequency of 0 is 3, so
this is 3; then frequency of 1

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:43.530
is 3, so 3; frequency of 2 is 2.

00:10:43.530 --> 00:10:44.220
OK.

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:48.540
And then you need to
locate 5 in this thing.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:52.590
Because your slot
is 5, so 5 is here.

00:10:52.590 --> 00:10:55.410
So this slot was 5, and
then s is equal to 1

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:57.210
because this lies in the slot.

00:10:57.210 --> 00:10:59.700
And then you do this
calculation-- block_id

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:03.460
times frequency plus
slot minus cumulative s.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:07.180
So block_id was
14 times frequency

00:11:07.180 --> 00:11:22.345
of 1, which is 3 plus slot, 5,
minus cumulative of 1, 3, 44.

00:11:30.350 --> 00:11:33.130
Any questions?

00:11:33.130 --> 00:11:38.670
So one quick thing is again
always focus on the main step.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.980
So main thing here
is this step, really.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:47.985
So what this is doing really
is x next is x multiplied by--

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:06.130
So you're doing x divided by
the probability of x really,

00:12:06.130 --> 00:12:12.070
and all of this is doing x
multiplied by probability of x.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:13.570
So that is the main operation.

00:12:13.570 --> 00:12:19.340
That is what gives you the
log 1/px result and so on.

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:23.050
So just remember that
that is the core lesson.

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:24.940
Please watch the
lecture from last time

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.670
or do the last question
in the homework

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:30.955
if you want to learn
more about rANS.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:36.290
OK.

00:12:36.290 --> 00:12:37.540
Here I will ask for your help.

00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:43.060
So the question is, when do
you want to use ANS as opposed

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.500
to arithmetic?

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:45.730
So first part.

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.370
Your application requires
the best compression ratio

00:12:48.370 --> 00:12:52.000
and you're willing to sacrifice
encoding or decoding speeds.

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.310
So what do you think
should be the answer here?

00:12:54.310 --> 00:12:59.148
Is the ANS the best
answer or something else?

00:12:59.148 --> 00:13:03.920
[INAUDIBLE]

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:04.420
OK.

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:08.460
I see some heads shaking.

00:13:08.460 --> 00:13:13.380
So here actually arithmetic
is typically the best answer.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:17.280
Talk to us later if you
thought differently.

00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.380
Your application
requires extremely fast

00:13:19.380 --> 00:13:21.840
encoding/decoding and you
are willing to sacrifice.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:23.490
Here the answer is
clear, it's Huffman.

00:13:23.490 --> 00:13:25.980
If you look at that table
from a few slides ago,

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:29.550
you can't beat Huffman at speed.

00:13:29.550 --> 00:13:32.640
It's just so simple.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:35.130
Your application requires
adaptive decoding.

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:38.070
So we haven't talked as much
about this adaptive stuff yet.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:41.550
I think we mentioned
it in class.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:45.060
We will talk about it
in the next lecture.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:47.640
Again, talk to us
if you are confused.

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:51.900
But here really the best
answer is arithmetic coding.

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.160
I think this was in one of the
slides in the last lecture.

00:13:55.160 --> 00:13:57.630
But you will understand
what we mean by adaptive

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:00.560
in the next lecture.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:02.600
So the last two, you
care about achieving

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:05.990
close-to-optimal compression
but also want good speed.

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:11.300
Here, I hope some of you
thought ANS was the answer

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:12.960
and yes, indeed it is.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:15.770
And the last one is you're
working with a modern processor

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:18.530
and want to exploit parallel
processing while still

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:21.140
achieving close to
optimal compression.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:23.330
So this is the same
stuff that I was

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:25.610
talking about, single
instruction, multiple data.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:28.610
These are special
instructions on the modern x86

00:14:28.610 --> 00:14:29.960
like the Intel machines.

00:14:29.960 --> 00:14:32.810
Also similar on the
ARM machines where

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:35.900
if you are doing the same
operation again and again,

00:14:35.900 --> 00:14:38.450
you can parallelize
that operation.

00:14:38.450 --> 00:14:40.820
And it's very interesting
the way they do it with rANS.

00:14:40.820 --> 00:14:42.290
I will not talk
about it too much,

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:46.850
but the basic idea is that
you have your symbols--

00:14:46.850 --> 00:14:48.920
symbol 1, symbol 2,
symbol, and so on.

00:14:48.920 --> 00:14:53.330
What you do is you
compress symbols 1, 3, 5, 7

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:58.490
and symbols 2, 4, 6, 8 in
parallel, basically, which

00:14:58.490 --> 00:15:00.980
is very, very suitable for
these modern processors who

00:15:00.980 --> 00:15:02.960
can do these two
operations in parallel.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:06.770
Basically, you get 2x speed
up without using two cores.

00:15:06.770 --> 00:15:08.900
You need to write the
code in a proper way,

00:15:08.900 --> 00:15:15.590
but I think there
was a paper I linked

00:15:15.590 --> 00:15:16.640
if you want to read more.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.030
This paper.

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.680
So this paper should
give you a sense

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.020
of that parallel
encoding/decoding.

00:15:28.900 --> 00:15:29.400
OK.

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.470
So here are the last two,
what are the answers?

00:15:31.470 --> 00:15:33.330
