00:18:28.370 --> 00:18:30.770
So we read all these
entropy coding and so on,

00:18:30.770 --> 00:18:33.440
so I thought let's try
to compress this book.

00:18:33.440 --> 00:18:39.940
And I basically call
this get_entropy function

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:43.790
to get the empirical entropy.

00:18:43.790 --> 00:18:46.990
You compute the frequency
of each of the letters

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:48.370
and you do it.

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:53.380
And I got this 199 kilobytes
where the original file

00:18:53.380 --> 00:18:55.090
was 387 kilobytes.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:56.030
So that's good.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:18:58.120
That's roughly a 2x compression.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:18:58.870
We are happy.

00:18:58.870 --> 00:18:59.740
No.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:00.280
We are not.

00:19:00.280 --> 00:19:03.100
Because then I try to compress
it with gzip and bzip2 which

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:05.613
are some standard compressors.

00:19:05.613 --> 00:19:07.030
They're not even
state-of-the-art.

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:12.070
They're pretty old, 20-year-old
compressors, 30-year-old.

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:14.800
And they get much
smaller than entropy.

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.680
But didn't we study that
entropy is the best you can do?

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:21.070
Entropy is the lower bound on
any prefix tree, any lossless

00:19:21.070 --> 00:19:21.910
compressor.

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:29.990
So any suggestions?

00:19:29.990 --> 00:19:32.290
Why is entropy not
the best you can do?

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:35.320
Actually, some of you might
have noticed it in homework 1.

00:19:35.320 --> 00:19:36.820
I think we had a
question where we

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:40.028
asked you to do gzip on a file
and you saw that it was better

00:19:40.028 --> 00:19:40.570
than Huffman.

00:19:43.563 --> 00:19:44.980
I think it has to
do with the fact

00:19:44.980 --> 00:19:49.972
that each letter you
can look at the property

00:19:49.972 --> 00:19:50.930
given the first letter.

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:53.720
For example, if you
have the letter T,

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:57.250
there is a high probability this
is going to be H, for example.

00:19:57.250 --> 00:20:00.940
So maybe with that, you
get a better performance.

00:20:00.940 --> 00:20:02.200
Yep, yep, yep.

00:20:02.200 --> 00:20:05.980
So a very, very good
answer which is that--

00:20:05.980 --> 00:20:07.980
all of this is based
on iid assumption.

00:20:07.980 --> 00:20:09.892
For the first eight
lectures-- first seven

00:20:09.892 --> 00:20:11.100
lectures we just studied iid.

00:20:11.100 --> 00:20:13.695
So there is a risk that we
assume the world is just iid,

00:20:13.695 --> 00:20:15.570
everything is independent
of everything else.

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:16.140
It is not.

00:20:16.140 --> 00:20:17.610
We know that.

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:22.200
So the example was if your
previous letter is a T,

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:24.960
you know with very high
probability or some probability

00:20:24.960 --> 00:20:29.520
that the next letter might
be a H. You have the, then,

00:20:29.520 --> 00:20:30.390
this, that.

00:20:30.390 --> 00:20:33.045
A lot of words like that.

00:20:33.045 --> 00:20:34.770
Even a stronger example.

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:36.875
If your previous
letter was a Q, you

00:20:36.875 --> 00:20:38.250
know with even
higher probability

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:43.110
that the next letter
is going to be a U.

00:20:43.110 --> 00:20:48.990
Or if you are doing English and
you had a start of a sentence,

00:20:48.990 --> 00:20:51.210
you know that the first
letter will be capitalized

00:20:51.210 --> 00:20:53.550
and then you have small letters.

00:20:53.550 --> 00:20:55.110
So there are a lot
of things you know

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:59.580
about English, which are not
explained by this iid property.

00:20:59.580 --> 00:21:04.650
iid would just say that you have
15% E's and 10% T's and so on.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:07.770
But it says nothing about if the
previous symbol was something,

00:21:07.770 --> 00:21:09.060
what is the next symbol?

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:15.420
So again, iid means independent
and identically distributed,

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:17.010
which says that
each of your symbols

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:20.280
is independent of
each other and they

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.020
have the same distribution.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:24.630
But what you will see is that
given your past, actually

00:21:24.630 --> 00:21:27.810
the distribution keeps
changing from symbol to symbol.

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.130
So that's the first thing.

00:21:29.130 --> 00:21:30.930
And there are other reasons.

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:32.850
If you just compute
the entropy on a big--

00:21:32.850 --> 00:21:36.090
you just concatenate two files.

00:21:36.090 --> 00:21:40.590
You take a Java code and you
take the Sherlock Holmes book

00:21:40.590 --> 00:21:42.990
and you concatenate them.

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:45.030
And you compute the
entropy on both of them,

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:47.550
the entropy will be a
mix of the two entropies.

00:21:47.550 --> 00:21:50.100
It's not the optimal thing to
do for either the Java code

00:21:50.100 --> 00:21:53.310
or for the Sherlock Holmes file.

00:21:53.310 --> 00:21:57.330
So for these two reasons,
but really the first one

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:59.040
is what we'll focus on for now.

00:22:03.590 --> 00:22:06.420
So we'll look at non-iid data.

00:22:06.420 --> 00:22:08.670
We'll look at
real-life data now.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.620
So we'll look at real-life data.

00:22:10.620 --> 00:22:15.310
We will do two things--
one is non-iid data

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:17.710
and the other is data
whose distribution

00:22:17.710 --> 00:22:19.930
we don't know a priori.

00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:22.000
So in homework 1, you
already saw an example

00:22:22.000 --> 00:22:23.890
where you were given a
file and you computed

00:22:23.890 --> 00:22:26.410
the empirical entropy,
you encoded with Huffman

00:22:26.410 --> 00:22:28.870
coding you stored the codebook.

00:22:28.870 --> 00:22:30.160
So that is one way to do it.

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:32.020
There are other ways
we will look at where--

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:41.000
it is easy to say that I get
50% 0's and 25% 1's and 25% 2's,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:43.870
but who has seen a real
distribution like that?

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:46.450
Oftentimes when you
start with a data source,

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:49.505
it's very complicated and you
don't know the distribution

00:22:49.505 --> 00:22:50.005
often.

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.940
And we have been
talking just about text,

00:22:56.940 --> 00:22:59.580
but think of images.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:05.920
If I'm compressing
this image, this slide,

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:08.020
it's very correlated,
the nearby pixels.

00:23:08.020 --> 00:23:10.510
If the nearby pixel is white
it's more likely to be white.

00:23:10.510 --> 00:23:16.690
The black guys are all bunched
up together, the black pixels.

00:23:16.690 --> 00:23:20.590
If you have a more scenery
sort of thing, you have sky,

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:22.540
the blue colors are nearby.

00:23:22.540 --> 00:23:24.730
So there is some
correlation nearby.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:31.030
If you think of video, video
is also like nearby frames.

00:23:31.030 --> 00:23:33.880
Even if there is motion,
there is a slow motion.

00:23:33.880 --> 00:23:35.560
So nearby frames
look very similar.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:38.470
So again, there is some
dependence, tables.

00:23:38.470 --> 00:23:41.050
And anything in real life,
everything is non-iid.

00:23:41.050 --> 00:23:44.980
Very rarely you get from nature
or from real life or thing

00:23:44.980 --> 00:23:46.140
that's iid.

00:23:49.920 --> 00:23:51.735
Any questions on
the motivation part?

