00:25:00.043 --> 00:25:02.210
You will see that all of
these tools we have learned

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:04.790
are very, very
useful in this case

00:25:04.790 --> 00:25:08.420
also, also for
lossy compression.

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:16.910
How many of you have
seen Markov chains

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:19.220
in a previous probability
course or information

00:25:19.220 --> 00:25:20.660
theory or any course at all?

00:25:24.340 --> 00:25:24.840
OK.

00:25:24.840 --> 00:25:26.340
A few of you have, some haven't.

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:28.538
It's fine.

00:25:28.538 --> 00:25:30.330
You don't need to know
too many properties,

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:33.900
but we will need some
basics, which I will cover.

00:25:38.550 --> 00:25:40.440
So recall that if you
have a block like you

00:25:40.440 --> 00:25:43.790
went through Un,
in the iid case,

00:25:43.790 --> 00:25:47.300
the joint probability
of all of these guys

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:51.750
is the product of
the probabilities.

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.660
But in general if you
have done chain rule,

00:25:55.660 --> 00:25:57.310
you can't just write
it as a product.

00:25:57.310 --> 00:26:01.900
You need to do this chain
rule where it's like PU1,

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:08.680
U2 is PU1 multiplied by PU2
given U1, that sort of thing.

00:26:08.680 --> 00:26:11.260
And then you can
keep extending this.

00:26:11.260 --> 00:26:13.300
So just the chain rule.

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:19.690
If you've forgotten this
part of your probability,

00:26:19.690 --> 00:26:23.470
we can share some material.

00:26:23.470 --> 00:26:33.130
So we will just go all in
finite-length sequences now.

00:26:33.130 --> 00:26:37.860
So given some alphabet
U, a binary maybe

00:26:37.860 --> 00:26:42.020
or something else, alphabet
maybe like English alphabet,

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:44.570
you can create a
stochastic process.

00:26:44.570 --> 00:26:48.690
So a stochastic process
or a random process

00:26:48.690 --> 00:26:51.210
is just any sequence
of random variables

00:26:51.210 --> 00:26:52.940
with arbitrary dependence.

00:26:55.490 --> 00:26:58.248
And it is characterized
by basically for every n,

00:26:58.248 --> 00:27:00.290
you have to describe the
probability distribution

00:27:00.290 --> 00:27:00.998
for that n-tuple.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:06.100
So in the iid case,
this is very simple.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:08.920
In the iid case for any
n, this thing factorizes.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:10.900
So you write the probability
of U1 through Un,

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:13.600
it's just the product
of U1, U2, U3, Un.

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:16.000
But in general if
you don't assume iid,

00:27:16.000 --> 00:27:18.250
this could have
arbitrary distributions.

00:27:18.250 --> 00:27:22.030
You have just a bunch
of big, big distribution

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:24.700
tables sitting with you.

00:27:24.700 --> 00:27:27.580
And this is very, very general.

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:30.550
And we won't to talk about this.

00:27:30.550 --> 00:27:34.373
This is way too general to
be of much use in theory.

00:27:34.373 --> 00:27:36.790
But you need to know there is
a notion of a random process

00:27:36.790 --> 00:27:40.270
where it's just a sequence of
random variables with arbitrary

00:27:40.270 --> 00:27:42.250
dependence on 1's.

00:27:42.250 --> 00:27:45.040
A random variable might depend
on something that's a million

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:46.240
before it.

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:47.290
Anything can happen.

00:27:50.150 --> 00:27:53.150
I will pause for questions
after a couple of these.

00:27:53.150 --> 00:27:55.430
Then we have stationary
stochastic process

00:27:55.430 --> 00:27:59.780
or a stationary
process, which is

00:27:59.780 --> 00:28:03.470
a stochastic or a random
process that is time-invariant.

00:28:03.470 --> 00:28:05.870
So what it means
is that whether you

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:07.550
are at position 0 in
the process or you

00:28:07.550 --> 00:28:10.130
are at position 100 or
position 1,000 or million,

00:28:10.130 --> 00:28:11.990
the probabilities don't change.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:15.290
It's in a stationary
state in a way.

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:19.310
We look at some
examples, but if you

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:24.470
like to define it very precisely
what it says is that for any n

00:28:24.470 --> 00:28:28.460
and for any l, the
probability that U1 through Un

00:28:28.460 --> 00:28:29.420
is equal to something.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:32.030
It's the same as the
probability at Ul plus 1

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:34.460
to Ul plus 1 is
equal to something.

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:40.080
So this is just saying
that if you shift your--

00:28:40.080 --> 00:28:42.825
just an example, basically,
we are saying PU1,

00:28:42.825 --> 00:28:48.780
U2 will have the same
distribution as PU100 and U101.

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:51.500
So the same distribution.

00:28:51.500 --> 00:28:54.290
So this is an example of a--

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:57.170
a stationary process must
satisfy this sort of thing.

00:28:57.170 --> 00:28:58.940
Wherever you are in
your time series,

00:28:58.940 --> 00:29:00.710
it will have the same
distribution there.

00:29:00.710 --> 00:29:02.210
Same joint distribution.

00:29:06.410 --> 00:29:08.240
The general definition
is fine, but

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:10.670
in particular, specific
properties are the mean,

00:29:10.670 --> 00:29:14.420
variance, entropy, all of
these do not change with n.

00:29:14.420 --> 00:29:18.620
So what we are saying is the
expectation of U1 will be

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:21.570
the same as expectation
of U100, for example,

00:29:21.570 --> 00:29:26.420
or the entropy of U1 will be
the same as entropy of U100

00:29:26.420 --> 00:29:31.490
or the entropy of U1, U3 will
be the same as the entropy

00:29:31.490 --> 00:29:36.090
of U1,000 and U2002.

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:40.600
So this sort of properties.

00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:42.580
And even the stationary
distributions

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:44.620
are not necessarily very simple.

00:29:44.620 --> 00:29:47.350
They can have arbitrary
time dependence.

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:56.100
So in particular, U1 and
U1,000,000,000 can be

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:56.670
dependent.

00:29:56.670 --> 00:29:58.890
It's not like they
have to be independent.

00:29:58.890 --> 00:30:01.440
You can have very
far-reaching dependence.

00:30:06.010 --> 00:30:07.480
We will keep moving on.

00:30:07.480 --> 00:30:08.500
We'll look at examples.

00:30:08.500 --> 00:30:11.380
You never really need to deal
with like a fully general

00:30:11.380 --> 00:30:12.520
stationary process.

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:15.130
We'll get more and more
specific as time goes on.

00:30:19.810 --> 00:30:20.830
Any questions so far?

00:30:24.810 --> 00:30:27.300
So let's say you have
iid sequence which

00:30:27.300 --> 00:30:29.940
is a sequence of
fair iid coin tosses.

00:30:29.940 --> 00:30:32.460
So probability of head is
equal to probability of tail

00:30:32.460 --> 00:30:34.440
is equal to 1/2.

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:36.360
Can someone tell me
what is this thing?

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:38.550
The probability that
the first four tosses

00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:46.182
are in sequence H, T, T, H.

00:30:46.182 --> 00:30:49.050
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:49.550
Yeah.

00:30:49.550 --> 00:30:52.820
I'm handing 1 over 2 to 4, which
is correct because this is iid,

00:30:52.820 --> 00:30:56.420
so this is just like PU1
equal to H multiplied

00:30:56.420 --> 00:31:00.530
by PU2 equal to T, dot, dot.

00:31:00.530 --> 00:31:04.550
Can somebody tell if I put some
l here, so I just shift it,

00:31:04.550 --> 00:31:06.380
I'm looking further
in the future--

00:31:06.380 --> 00:31:10.910
maybe P101, 100, 200, and 304,
what is the probability now?

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:15.690
It's the same because
again, it's iid.

00:31:15.690 --> 00:31:18.578
And iid means independent
and identically distributed.

00:31:18.578 --> 00:31:20.370
So all of these have
the same distribution,

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.970
they're all independent.

00:31:22.970 --> 00:31:29.420
So you can show it somewhere
that iid implies stationary.

00:31:29.420 --> 00:31:30.290
So any iid.

00:31:30.290 --> 00:31:32.210
All the processes
you have seen so far

00:31:32.210 --> 00:31:34.490
are all stationary
because they are iid.

00:31:34.490 --> 00:31:37.100
iid is a very special
case of stationary.

00:31:40.610 --> 00:31:44.600
Now we'll look at a more
complex thing, a Markov process.

00:31:49.220 --> 00:31:55.010
So a Markov process if
you have not seen one

00:31:55.010 --> 00:32:00.760
is you can define it in terms
of one-step probability.

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:03.730
So if you know U1 you can define
the probability distribution

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:07.000
for U2 conditioned on U1.

00:32:07.000 --> 00:32:12.370
And then if you have U3 then
you can define U3 given U2.

00:32:12.370 --> 00:32:17.840
So the real property
here is that Un

00:32:17.840 --> 00:32:31.520
is independent of Un minus
2 to U1 given Un minus 1.

00:32:31.520 --> 00:32:34.970
So what this says is that if you
know the just previous symbol,

00:32:34.970 --> 00:32:38.420
if you know Un minus 1,
then your future and past

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:39.680
are independent.

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.500
So the only dependent
is at one symbol level.

00:32:42.500 --> 00:32:44.300
So if you know the
previous symbol,

00:32:44.300 --> 00:32:45.630
that's all you need to know.

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:48.005
I tell you the last million
symbols, that doesn't matter,

00:32:48.005 --> 00:32:49.970
you only need to
know the last symbol.

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:52.400
That is the property
of a Markov process.

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:56.705
And I think it's easiest
explained through this diagram.

00:32:59.860 --> 00:33:04.400
So we start with U1 which is
uniformly drawn from 0, 1, 2.

00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:06.560
So let's say you sample 0.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:09.960
Let's say U1 is 0.

00:33:09.960 --> 00:33:13.230
So once you are at
0 these arrows show

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:14.820
what is the next symbol.

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.940
So if you are 0 you see an
arrow with 0.5 probability going

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:21.610
to 1, and you see an
arrow with 0.5 probability

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:23.230
is staying at 0.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:27.460
So that means that if U1 is
0 then U2 can either be 0

00:33:27.460 --> 00:33:31.020
or it can be 1, that
half of probability.

00:33:31.020 --> 00:33:34.310
So it's a one-step process.

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:35.480
I wrote it in another way.

00:33:35.480 --> 00:33:38.390
Maybe this will be easier
for some to understand.

00:33:38.390 --> 00:33:41.720
U1 is again sampled
uniformly and UI plus 1.

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:47.780
So the next one is UI plus
Zi mod 3, where Zi is 0/1.

00:33:47.780 --> 00:33:51.140
So basically, we are saying if
you are at 0, you can go to 0

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:52.640
or you can go to 1.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:55.430
If you are at 1 you can go
to 1 or you can go to 2.

00:33:55.430 --> 00:33:57.950
If you are at 2 you can go
to 2 or you can go to 0.

00:33:57.950 --> 00:33:58.850
So mod 3.

00:33:58.850 --> 00:34:02.510
So you round back to this.

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:05.180
This is a very simple Markov
process where at every step

00:34:05.180 --> 00:34:09.739
you either go to yourself
or you go to 1 plus U.

00:34:09.739 --> 00:34:12.980
And there are various
ways to represent it.

00:34:12.980 --> 00:34:15.080
You don't really need
to worry too much,

00:34:15.080 --> 00:34:17.420
but maybe you have seen
one of these, not the other

00:34:17.420 --> 00:34:18.770
so that's why I made all three.

00:34:18.770 --> 00:34:23.030
So this just shows this thing as
a table where if you are at 0,

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:26.300
then the next guy can be 0 or
1 with 0.5, 0.5 probability.

00:34:26.300 --> 00:34:29.389
If you are at 1, then the next
guy can be like this, too.

00:34:29.389 --> 00:34:31.670
So that's another
representation.

00:34:31.670 --> 00:34:33.350
It's called a transition matrix.

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:36.570
Or you can write it in the form
of an equation, which is not

00:34:36.570 --> 00:34:38.850
always possible,
or you can write it

00:34:38.850 --> 00:34:40.739
in this graphical manner.

00:34:40.739 --> 00:34:42.510
We'll mostly make
this graphical manner

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:44.129
for the rest of the lecture.

00:34:48.020 --> 00:34:48.520
OK.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:55.510
Now let me do this math and stop
me whenever you don't like it.

00:35:11.622 --> 00:35:13.080
Can somebody tell
me what this rule

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.480
is called, this step
that I just performed?

00:35:31.380 --> 00:35:31.880
OK.

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:35.770
So this is just called
marginalization,

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:38.640
the opposite of this step.

00:35:38.640 --> 00:35:41.970
If you add up U1 over
all its values then

00:35:41.970 --> 00:35:43.590
you're just left
with U2, basically.

00:35:43.590 --> 00:35:46.650
So that's a marginalization.

00:35:46.650 --> 00:35:47.910
We'll have a quiz question.

00:35:47.910 --> 00:35:54.080
So I hope this example will
help you do that very easily.

00:35:54.080 --> 00:36:01.240
Now, let's do it this way--

00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.310
PU2 equal to 0.

00:36:03.310 --> 00:36:06.490
This is a very simple topology
if you already know this,

00:36:06.490 --> 00:36:08.060
but we will do it.

00:36:08.060 --> 00:36:08.560
OK.

00:36:08.560 --> 00:36:10.560
Can somebody tell what I
did in the second step?

00:36:18.490 --> 00:36:21.545
This is just the chain rule.

00:36:24.240 --> 00:36:26.100
These are not hard
questions, I just

00:36:26.100 --> 00:36:29.460
want to make sure we're
very comfortable with this.

00:36:29.460 --> 00:36:33.150
Now let's just write it
down and quickly compute it.

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:47.870
U1 equal to 1, U2 equal
to 0 given U1 equal to 1.

00:36:47.870 --> 00:36:53.410
U1 equal to 2, U2 equal
to 0, U1 equal to 2.

00:36:55.892 --> 00:36:57.350
Now I'm going to
ask for your help.

00:36:57.350 --> 00:36:59.390
Please help me fill
in each of these.

00:36:59.390 --> 00:37:03.995
So what is U1 equal to 0,
probability that U1 equal to 0?

00:37:03.995 --> 00:37:05.940
It's 1 over 3.

00:37:05.940 --> 00:37:06.670
Yeah, it's 1/3.

00:37:06.670 --> 00:37:07.170
Why?

00:37:07.170 --> 00:37:10.230
Because U1 is given
as uniform 0, 1, 2.

00:37:10.230 --> 00:37:12.450
So let me just fill
in all three of these

00:37:12.450 --> 00:37:16.220
because we know all
three of these are 1/3.

00:37:16.220 --> 00:37:16.720
OK.

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:19.840
Somebody tell me what is
probability U2 equal to 0 given

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:20.860
U1 equal to 0.

00:37:20.860 --> 00:37:23.110
Anybody else?

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:24.650
Half.

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:25.150
Half.

00:37:25.150 --> 00:37:25.650
Right.

00:37:25.650 --> 00:37:30.950
Half which is correct because
it's basically this branch.

00:37:30.950 --> 00:37:34.200
If you are at 0 then the next
one is 0 with probability half.

00:37:34.200 --> 00:37:36.330
What is the probability
that U2 equal to 0

00:37:36.330 --> 00:37:37.590
given that U1 equal to 1?

00:37:40.470 --> 00:37:41.400
0.

00:37:41.400 --> 00:37:43.260
Yeah, I heard 0 which
is again correct.

00:37:43.260 --> 00:37:43.760
Why?

00:37:43.760 --> 00:37:47.460
Because if you are at
1, so you are here,

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:50.790
then the probability
of going to 0 is 0.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:53.670
There is no way
you go back to 0.

00:37:53.670 --> 00:37:55.260
And again this guy is half.

00:37:55.260 --> 00:37:56.790
So after you compute
all of this you

00:37:56.790 --> 00:38:00.000
will get this thing
is equal to 1/3.

00:38:13.503 --> 00:38:14.970
[INAUDIBLE]

00:38:14.970 --> 00:38:15.991
Right.

00:38:15.991 --> 00:38:22.580
[INAUDIBLE]

00:38:22.580 --> 00:38:23.080
Right.

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:28.660
So a quick note is
that for a Markov chain

00:38:28.660 --> 00:38:36.840
if you can show that U2 and U1
have same distribution, that

00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:40.020
is enough to show
that it's stationary.

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:44.230
So this implies that it's
a stationary Markov chain.

00:38:44.230 --> 00:38:48.850
And you can easily check that it
is stationary here because see,

00:38:48.850 --> 00:38:50.350
U1 had a uniform distribution.

00:38:50.350 --> 00:38:52.930
And if you compute U2
equal to 0, U2 equal to 1,

00:38:52.930 --> 00:38:55.780
U2 equal to 2, you will again
get the uniform distribution.

00:38:55.780 --> 00:38:58.480
So the distribution
didn't change in one step.

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:00.610
And then just by
induction, you can

00:39:00.610 --> 00:39:02.770
show that it won't change
in the next step also.

00:39:02.770 --> 00:39:04.840
And then you can show that
all joint distribution

00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:05.590
nothing changes.

00:39:05.590 --> 00:39:07.450
So it's stationary chain.

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:10.330
At every step,
each of these guys

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:12.610
have 1/3 probability
of being in that state.

00:39:16.440 --> 00:39:18.750
If you don't fully
appreciate it, it's fine.

00:39:18.750 --> 00:39:22.410
But you will have
questions in quiz, so just

00:39:22.410 --> 00:39:25.230
know that this can be done.

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:33.080
This I will leave as a homework.

00:39:33.080 --> 00:39:34.730
You will have a
quiz question which

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:38.420
is very similar, which is that
if you start with probability

00:39:38.420 --> 00:39:39.290
U1 equal--

00:39:39.290 --> 00:39:42.678
sorry, if you assume
that U1 is equal to 0--

00:39:42.678 --> 00:39:43.970
actually, can somebody help me?

00:39:43.970 --> 00:39:45.920
Is the process still stationary?

00:39:45.920 --> 00:39:47.960
Can somebody tell me
what is the probability

00:39:47.960 --> 00:39:51.140
that U2 equal to
0, if U1 is known

00:39:51.140 --> 00:39:53.830
to be 0 with probability 1?

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:55.590
Half.

00:39:55.590 --> 00:39:56.090
Yeah.

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:58.910
I hear that U2 is 0
is probability half.

00:39:58.910 --> 00:40:00.650
Why?

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:03.260
Because U1 you know
definitely is that 0.

00:40:03.260 --> 00:40:06.530
So U2 will be back here
with probability half.

00:40:06.530 --> 00:40:08.390
So is this process stationary?

00:40:11.450 --> 00:40:13.790
It's not because clearly
the probability has changed.

00:40:13.790 --> 00:40:15.582
Stationary means that
the probability stays

00:40:15.582 --> 00:40:17.450
constant across time steps.

00:40:17.450 --> 00:40:19.580
So it's important that when
you have a Markov chain

00:40:19.580 --> 00:40:21.650
you start with the
correct distribution.

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:24.410
If you don't, then it
won't be stationary.

00:40:27.805 --> 00:40:29.260
[INAUDIBLE]

00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:31.247
Yeah.

00:40:31.247 --> 00:40:32.080
That's good to know.

00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:33.970
I think many of you
have done probability,

00:40:33.970 --> 00:40:37.690
so hopefully nothing new here.

00:40:37.690 --> 00:40:39.730
This is probably the
more interesting question

00:40:39.730 --> 00:40:41.380
from a compression perspective.

00:40:44.200 --> 00:40:47.490
So given the sequence
that we just made,

00:40:47.490 --> 00:40:52.200
can somebody suggest me a way
to make it into an iid sequence?

00:40:52.200 --> 00:40:55.050
This is a Markov sequence,
it has dependence,

00:40:55.050 --> 00:40:58.500
can you help me make it
into an iid sequence?

00:41:03.210 --> 00:41:05.520
And maybe I will
write it in a way that

00:41:05.520 --> 00:41:08.400
will be easier to understand.

00:41:08.400 --> 00:41:14.670
So you have U1 then you have U1
plus Z1, where plus is modulo

00:41:14.670 --> 00:41:18.050
3, so we want to write that.

00:41:18.050 --> 00:41:26.350
And then U2 plus Z2
then U3 plus Z3, dot,

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:30.445
dot, dot, where Zi our
iid Bernoulli half.

00:41:33.260 --> 00:41:36.410
So if I write it in
this way, can somebody

00:41:36.410 --> 00:41:39.365
suggest how can I convert
this into iid sequence?

00:41:49.644 --> 00:41:55.050
This is my U1, this
is my U2, U3, U4.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.230
Yeah.

00:41:57.230 --> 00:41:59.780
Subtract the
consecutive numbers.

00:41:59.780 --> 00:42:00.690
Yeah.

00:42:00.690 --> 00:42:01.190
Very good.

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:03.110
So you just subtract them.

00:42:03.110 --> 00:42:04.070
You just subtract them.

00:42:04.070 --> 00:42:08.780
So you take U2 minus
U1, which is Z1,

00:42:08.780 --> 00:42:12.952
and then U3 minus U2,
which is Z2, and so on.

00:42:12.952 --> 00:42:13.910
You just subtract them.

00:42:13.910 --> 00:42:15.110
Again, modulo 3.

00:42:15.110 --> 00:42:17.075
And when you subtract
them you get these Z1,

00:42:17.075 --> 00:42:20.720
Z2 guys which are iid because,
by definition, the way we

00:42:20.720 --> 00:42:22.460
define this is like this.

00:42:22.460 --> 00:42:26.660
So even though originally the
process looked more complicated

00:42:26.660 --> 00:42:27.890
it had dependence.

00:42:27.890 --> 00:42:30.200
If you just subtract
the consecutive ones

00:42:30.200 --> 00:42:31.220
it becomes iid.

00:42:33.940 --> 00:42:36.468
It's not always this
simple, but oftentimes you

00:42:36.468 --> 00:42:39.010
will see that you can do simple
transformations on your data.

00:42:39.010 --> 00:42:40.510
You start with a
stationary process.

00:42:40.510 --> 00:42:42.580
Maybe you start with a
nonstationary process

00:42:42.580 --> 00:42:45.580
and you just do some simple
tricks it becomes iid,

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:47.230
and then you apply
your favorite--

00:42:47.230 --> 00:42:49.843
Huffman coding, ANS,
or whatever you like.

00:42:49.843 --> 00:42:51.760
And we'll see this again
and again, especially

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:53.260
in the lossy compression part.

00:42:53.260 --> 00:42:58.060
So this is a very simple
transform in a way.

00:42:58.060 --> 00:43:00.025
It's like delta
coding or diff coding.

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:04.085
Any questions so far?

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:12.030
OK.

00:43:12.030 --> 00:43:15.030
So we will just make
it more general.

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:18.070
And this will be
somewhat important.

00:43:18.070 --> 00:43:20.110
We will look at codes
for this, but basically,

00:43:20.110 --> 00:43:22.990
the idea is you can generalize
this Markov thing, where

00:43:22.990 --> 00:43:27.670
the Markov thing we said was
given the previous symbol,

00:43:27.670 --> 00:43:30.040
you don't have any dependence
on the further past.

00:43:30.040 --> 00:43:34.090
Kth order Markov means that
your current symbol just depends

00:43:34.090 --> 00:43:35.470
on the previous k symbols.

00:43:35.470 --> 00:43:37.690
If you know the
previous k symbols,

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:39.070
that is all you need to know.

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:42.190
Knowing the further away
past doesn't matter.

00:43:42.190 --> 00:43:45.970
So if you like to think
in terms of prediction,

00:43:45.970 --> 00:43:48.910
so what we are
saying is Un minus 1

00:43:48.910 --> 00:43:57.180
through Un minus k is
enough to predict Un.

00:43:57.180 --> 00:43:59.670
Knowing things even
before n minus k

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:03.330
won't really help you in
predicting Un better or knowing

00:44:03.330 --> 00:44:06.270
the distribution better.

00:44:06.270 --> 00:44:08.400
Let me just read
through the definition.

00:44:08.400 --> 00:44:12.030
So a kth Markov source is
defined by the condition--

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:17.080
probability of Un given
its entire past is

00:44:17.080 --> 00:44:21.910
same as the probability of Un
just given the last k symbols

00:44:21.910 --> 00:44:23.420
for every n.

00:44:23.420 --> 00:44:25.190
Inwards, the
conditional probability

00:44:25.190 --> 00:44:27.830
of Un given the entire
past depends only

00:44:27.830 --> 00:44:31.580
on the past k symbols,
or more precisely Un

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:35.660
is independent of the past older
than k symbols given the last k

00:44:35.660 --> 00:44:36.650
symbols.

00:44:36.650 --> 00:44:39.800
So knowing the last k symbols
is the best predictor for Un.

00:44:42.420 --> 00:44:44.072
So we saw that in
stationary sources.

00:44:44.072 --> 00:44:45.780
You could have arbitrary
time dependence,

00:44:45.780 --> 00:44:47.730
but in kth order Markov
source you have just

00:44:47.730 --> 00:44:50.490
passed k symbols you depend on.

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:53.910
So if you are generating this,
you take the last k sequence,

00:44:53.910 --> 00:44:57.375
you generate the next one,
then the last k generates,

00:44:57.375 --> 00:44:59.590
and so on.

00:44:59.590 --> 00:45:01.530
So most practical
stationary sources

00:45:01.530 --> 00:45:05.940
can be approximated well with
a finite kth order model.

00:45:05.940 --> 00:45:09.420
And you can go higher k and then
you get a better approximation

00:45:09.420 --> 00:45:10.500
with diminishing returns.

00:45:14.400 --> 00:45:16.560
Any questions on kth
order Markov sources?

00:45:16.560 --> 00:45:18.750
It's a generalization
of first-order

00:45:18.750 --> 00:45:20.670
like the normal Markov
source we just saw.

00:45:23.460 --> 00:45:30.285
When we just say Markov, we
usually mean first order.

00:45:32.800 --> 00:45:35.440
And when we say iid
we mean zeroth order

00:45:35.440 --> 00:45:40.650
because iid is you
just depend on nobody.

00:45:40.650 --> 00:45:42.780
So k is 0.

00:45:42.780 --> 00:45:45.135
You don't need to know any
past to predict the future.

00:45:54.380 --> 00:45:54.880
OK.

00:45:54.880 --> 00:45:58.480
Let's quickly look
at a nonexample of--

00:45:58.480 --> 00:46:01.950
I guess I told you
the answer, but--

00:46:01.950 --> 00:46:05.280
so you are at a bus stop
which is reasonably regular,

00:46:05.280 --> 00:46:08.280
buses arrive at roughly
15-minute frequency.

00:46:08.280 --> 00:46:09.990
And these are let's
say the bus timings--

00:46:09.990 --> 00:46:15.000
4:16, 4:28, 4:46, 5:02, is
this a stationary process?

00:46:15.000 --> 00:46:17.250
Is the sequence of
times, bus arrival times,

00:46:17.250 --> 00:46:19.340
is this stationary?

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:21.320
It is definitely
random because buses

00:46:21.320 --> 00:46:23.330
have some randomness
when they exactly arrive,

00:46:23.330 --> 00:46:24.680
but is it stationary?

00:46:24.680 --> 00:46:26.150
Is it iid, first of all?

00:46:26.150 --> 00:46:28.658
Is it stationary?

00:46:28.658 --> 00:46:30.482
[COUGH]

00:46:32.310 --> 00:46:35.040
So what are the properties
of stationary processes?

00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:38.040
Among other things,
we heard that the mean

00:46:38.040 --> 00:46:39.880
doesn't change with time.

00:46:39.880 --> 00:46:45.060
So do we think the average
bus arrival time keeps--

00:46:45.060 --> 00:46:48.240
if the first bus is expected
to arrive at 4:00 PM,

00:46:48.240 --> 00:46:52.800
is the second bus also
expected to arrive at 4:00 PM?

00:46:52.800 --> 00:46:53.300
No.

00:46:53.300 --> 00:46:55.670
It's expected to arrive
at 4:15 and the next one

00:46:55.670 --> 00:46:57.510
is 4:30 and so on.

00:46:57.510 --> 00:47:01.190
So this is not a
stationary process.

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:04.595
In particular, the mean
increases with time.

00:47:07.550 --> 00:47:10.610
Is that clear that
this is not stationary?

00:47:10.610 --> 00:47:13.850
because not all
buses are supposed

00:47:13.850 --> 00:47:15.740
to arrive at the
exact same time.

00:47:15.740 --> 00:47:17.220
OK.

00:47:17.220 --> 00:47:19.890
Can you convert it to
a stationary process

00:47:19.890 --> 00:47:21.870
by doing a simple
transformation?

00:47:28.506 --> 00:47:33.480
We need to see the right
difference between two halves.

00:47:33.480 --> 00:47:35.190
Yep, yep, yep, yep.

00:47:35.190 --> 00:47:39.280
So again, the answer is
just take the difference.

00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:41.800
This is surprisingly common.

00:47:41.800 --> 00:47:45.027
In real-life data, you
will see a lot of times--

00:47:45.027 --> 00:47:47.110
we will see a lot of
compressors, in this lecture,

00:47:47.110 --> 00:47:48.280
the next lecture, and so on.

00:47:51.315 --> 00:47:52.690
All of them will
work wonderfully

00:47:52.690 --> 00:47:54.040
on stationary sequences.

00:47:54.040 --> 00:47:56.200
But if you give it a
nonstationary like this,

00:47:56.200 --> 00:47:58.600
they just start failing.

00:47:58.600 --> 00:48:00.880
You take the best
state-of-the-art Zstandard

00:48:00.880 --> 00:48:03.820
or one of these compressors, you
give it a file which has 1, 2,

00:48:03.820 --> 00:48:06.670
3, 4, 5, 6, just a sequence
of numbers which is trivial

00:48:06.670 --> 00:48:07.215
to predict.

00:48:07.215 --> 00:48:09.940
If you know the previous
one you just do plus 1.

00:48:09.940 --> 00:48:12.620
And it will just do a terrible
job at compressing it.

00:48:12.620 --> 00:48:15.970
But if you just do this delta
coding or this diff difference

00:48:15.970 --> 00:48:18.190
between the consecutive
ones, it just

00:48:18.190 --> 00:48:21.900
becomes a sequence of very
similar-looking values

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:24.600
and then suddenly the
compression improves.

00:48:24.600 --> 00:48:29.526
So again, very important to--

00:48:29.526 --> 00:48:31.740
but that's why this
theory is important.

00:48:31.740 --> 00:48:34.260
You understand where do
these compressors do well,

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:35.910
where do they not do well.

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:37.740
And when they not
do well, can you

00:48:37.740 --> 00:48:40.440
convert it into a
different sequence?

00:48:40.440 --> 00:48:45.450
So here again you do
the delta or the diff.

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.220
You just take the difference.

00:48:47.220 --> 00:49:00.050
So you just do 428 minus
416, this is 12 minutes.

00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:05.250
If you have done the process
this is the interarrival time,

00:49:05.250 --> 00:49:10.170
usually, modeled as exponential
distribution in theory.

00:49:15.450 --> 00:49:15.950
OK.

00:49:15.950 --> 00:49:18.140
Let's move on to
information-theoretic

00:49:18.140 --> 00:49:21.290
quantities for non-iid
random variables

00:49:21.290 --> 00:49:22.550
if we have no questions.

00:49:26.200 --> 00:49:28.690
These you will see
in your homework.

00:49:28.690 --> 00:49:31.900
So, I guess, hopefully,
this will help you

00:49:31.900 --> 00:49:32.695
with the problems.

00:49:36.890 --> 00:49:40.100
So we saw the entropy.

00:49:40.100 --> 00:49:42.440
I hope everybody
remembers entropy of U

00:49:42.440 --> 00:49:50.020
was we did Pu log 1 over
Pu over all the u's.

00:49:50.020 --> 00:49:51.940
This was the entropy.

00:49:51.940 --> 00:49:54.580
So here we define the
conditional entropy of U

00:49:54.580 --> 00:49:57.220
given V. So now we have two
random variables U and V,

00:49:57.220 --> 00:49:59.650
which are not
necessarily independent.

00:49:59.650 --> 00:50:03.370
And this is defined
as this thing.

00:50:03.370 --> 00:50:06.810
So you can write this as
expectation of log 1 over Pu.

00:50:11.640 --> 00:50:14.720
So a very similar formula.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:16.530
Good.

00:50:16.530 --> 00:50:18.000
And then you can expand it out.

00:50:18.000 --> 00:50:21.760
We will try to do
one example in class.

00:50:21.760 --> 00:50:28.360
So this is just the definition
of the expectation, nothing

00:50:28.360 --> 00:50:29.500
very clever here.

00:50:29.500 --> 00:50:31.570
Second step, we
use the chain rule.

00:50:31.570 --> 00:50:34.330
So we do Pu, v is
like Pv multiplied

00:50:34.330 --> 00:50:41.480
by Pu given v. And then
you take this big thing

00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:45.260
and you can write it like this.

00:50:45.260 --> 00:50:47.530
So really we are
defining two quantities.

00:50:47.530 --> 00:50:55.240
We are defining two quantities.

00:50:55.240 --> 00:50:58.410
So one is this thing.

00:50:58.410 --> 00:51:09.680
This you can read as
conditional entropy of U given V

00:51:09.680 --> 00:51:12.320
is equal to v. So
if you know that V

00:51:12.320 --> 00:51:15.770
is equal to small v, then what
is the condition entropy of U?

00:51:15.770 --> 00:51:19.250
And this one is
nothing different

00:51:19.250 --> 00:51:20.960
from what you have already read.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:24.800
This is just the entropy if you
take this distribution, u given

00:51:24.800 --> 00:51:27.710
v equal to v. This one
you have already seen.

00:51:27.710 --> 00:51:30.770
So let's say, for
example, if v equal to v

00:51:30.770 --> 00:51:34.400
implies that u is
Bernoulli half,

00:51:34.400 --> 00:51:39.410
for example, then the entropy
of u given v equal to v,

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:42.550
somebody tell me
what is the answer.

00:51:42.550 --> 00:51:44.855
What is the entropy
of Bernoulli half?

00:51:44.855 --> 00:51:45.990
1.

00:51:45.990 --> 00:51:46.490
1.

00:51:46.490 --> 00:51:48.010
Right.

00:51:48.010 --> 00:51:49.780
So this one we are
very comfortable with.

00:51:49.780 --> 00:51:51.760
We have seen this a
million times this sort

00:51:51.760 --> 00:51:53.910
of simple thing--

00:51:53.910 --> 00:51:56.820
not a million but a few times.

00:51:56.820 --> 00:51:58.800
And then the second
thing we define

00:51:58.800 --> 00:52:08.810
is this, which is just
conditional entropy of u given

00:52:08.810 --> 00:52:16.400
v. So this is an average
of the above quantity.

00:52:16.400 --> 00:52:19.550
So this is on average, what is
the conditional entropy of u

00:52:19.550 --> 00:52:23.690
given v and therefore
it is defined

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:29.510
as Pv Hu v give equal to v?

00:52:29.510 --> 00:52:30.440
So what do we--

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.640
can somebody remind me
what was the intuitive

00:52:36.640 --> 00:52:37.525
meaning of entropy?

00:52:43.540 --> 00:52:45.220
If entropy is high,
what does that mean?

00:52:45.220 --> 00:52:48.910
[INAUDIBLE]

00:52:48.910 --> 00:52:49.675
Yep, yep.

00:52:49.675 --> 00:52:53.400
Like, uniform distribution
that [INAUDIBLE] the constant

00:52:53.400 --> 00:52:55.240
[INAUDIBLE].

00:52:55.240 --> 00:52:56.060
Exactly, exactly.

00:52:56.060 --> 00:52:56.560
Right.

00:52:56.560 --> 00:52:58.227
So entropy was a
measure of uncertainty,

00:52:58.227 --> 00:52:59.770
it was a measure of randomness.

00:52:59.770 --> 00:53:02.560
If it's a constant, it's 0
entropy, uniform, maximum

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:04.610
entropy.

00:53:04.610 --> 00:53:06.260
So can you guess
what is the meaning

00:53:06.260 --> 00:53:10.430
of the conditional entropy
of u given v equal to v

00:53:10.430 --> 00:53:14.390
based on what we know
about entropy already?

00:53:14.390 --> 00:53:16.760
So let's say knowing
that V equal to v,

00:53:16.760 --> 00:53:21.950
small v makes U
deterministic, then

00:53:21.950 --> 00:53:24.740
the entropy will be very
small, the conditional entropy.

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:28.340
So the conditional
entropy is really--

00:53:28.340 --> 00:53:31.700
again, the uncertainty
left in U or uncertainty

00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:36.200
in U given that U know
something about v.

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:44.230
So this thing is a
measure of uncertainty

00:53:44.230 --> 00:53:52.390
in U given that v is equal to v.
So if you know the value of v,

00:53:52.390 --> 00:53:55.510
what is the uncertainty in U?

00:53:55.510 --> 00:54:04.130
And this guy is the
average uncertainty

00:54:04.130 --> 00:54:10.330
in U given that
given v, basically.

00:54:10.330 --> 00:54:13.000
So on average if you know
v, how much uncertainty

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:14.950
do you have about U?

00:54:14.950 --> 00:54:16.870
So just think of an example.

00:54:16.870 --> 00:54:24.740
For example, you are playing
this murder mystery type

00:54:24.740 --> 00:54:27.530
of game where you have
suspicion on a bunch

00:54:27.530 --> 00:54:29.090
of different suspects.

00:54:29.090 --> 00:54:31.490
So that is U. U is
the variable that

00:54:31.490 --> 00:54:34.010
represents who is the murderer.

00:54:34.010 --> 00:54:37.220
And then you keep
getting specific clues.

00:54:37.220 --> 00:54:40.060
So maybe you get some
fingerprint clue.

00:54:40.060 --> 00:54:42.850
And maybe that will change
the uncertainty in who

00:54:42.850 --> 00:54:44.680
you think the murderer is.

00:54:44.680 --> 00:54:48.517
You will find that some clues
will increase your uncertainty.

00:54:48.517 --> 00:54:51.100
Earlier you thought, oh, no, I
think this guy is the murderer.

00:54:51.100 --> 00:54:54.340
But then you see this clue and
then it confuses you again.

00:54:54.340 --> 00:54:58.290
So what you will see
is that this quantity

00:54:58.290 --> 00:55:01.470
can be bigger than or smaller
than the entropy of U.

00:55:01.470 --> 00:55:03.450
Knowing a specific
piece of evidence

00:55:03.450 --> 00:55:06.660
can increase your entropy
or decrease your entropy.

00:55:06.660 --> 00:55:09.240
But you will see
that on average.

00:55:09.240 --> 00:55:11.160
Knowing more can't hurt you.

00:55:11.160 --> 00:55:13.380
The more you know
about v, it will never

00:55:13.380 --> 00:55:15.910
hurt you in terms of U.

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:17.820
So it will decrease
your uncertainty.

00:55:17.820 --> 00:55:20.430
So as you play this
murder game, over time

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:22.830
as you get more and more
clues, your uncertainty

00:55:22.830 --> 00:55:23.700
will keep reducing.

00:55:23.700 --> 00:55:25.867
And if you are a good
detective like Sherlock Holmes

00:55:25.867 --> 00:55:29.355
you will converge on
the correct culprit.

00:55:33.780 --> 00:55:34.920
Any questions?

00:55:40.040 --> 00:55:40.540
OK.

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:41.785
Let's try to do this example.

00:55:47.140 --> 00:55:49.650
So you are given
this distribution.

00:55:49.650 --> 00:55:53.100
This is a joint distribution
between u and v.

00:55:53.100 --> 00:55:57.540
So the way this is
written is like this cell

00:55:57.540 --> 00:56:02.550
is the probability
that u is 0 and v is 0.

00:56:02.550 --> 00:56:05.160
And this cell is u is 1, v is 1.

00:56:09.260 --> 00:56:12.620
So if you read this,
I would encourage

00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:16.010
you to do this
carefully at home,

00:56:16.010 --> 00:56:20.040
you will see that u is actually
uniformly distributed in 0, 1.

00:56:20.040 --> 00:56:24.652
And knowing that u is 0, so that
is the first row in that table,

00:56:24.652 --> 00:56:27.110
it means that v has
equal probability

00:56:27.110 --> 00:56:29.960
of being 0 or being 1.

00:56:29.960 --> 00:56:31.820
So what is the
formula we use here?

00:56:31.820 --> 00:56:33.410
It's like this guy--

00:56:33.410 --> 00:56:39.530
v equal to 0 given u equal
to 0 is simply Pu equal to 0,

00:56:39.530 --> 00:56:41.540
v equal to 0 divided
by probability

00:56:41.540 --> 00:56:47.680
that u equal to 0, which is 1/4
divided by 1/2 which is 1/2.

00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:54.060
And if u is known to be 1, then
you see v has no uncertainty,

00:56:54.060 --> 00:56:55.410
v is definitely 1.

00:56:55.410 --> 00:56:59.370
So if u is 1 then v is 1.

00:56:59.370 --> 00:57:00.630
This is simple stuff.

00:57:03.360 --> 00:57:04.770
Can we try this?

00:57:04.770 --> 00:57:07.935
So what is the entropy in v
given that u is equal to 0?

00:57:15.806 --> 00:57:17.220
So u is equal to 0.

00:57:17.220 --> 00:57:19.170
Entropy in v given
that u equal to 0.

00:57:19.170 --> 00:57:20.510
1.

00:57:20.510 --> 00:57:21.230
It's 1.

00:57:21.230 --> 00:57:21.730
Why?

00:57:21.730 --> 00:57:24.320
Because we saw
that if u is 0 then

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:25.670
v is simply Bernoulli half.

00:57:25.670 --> 00:57:27.290
So that has entropy 1.

00:57:27.290 --> 00:57:31.896
What about this guy, entropy
in v if u is equal to 1?

00:57:31.896 --> 00:57:33.760
It's 0.

00:57:33.760 --> 00:57:34.450
It's 0 again.

00:57:34.450 --> 00:57:35.290
Why is 0?

00:57:35.290 --> 00:57:37.540
Because this is deterministic.

00:57:37.540 --> 00:57:41.050
Knowing u equal to 1 definitely
tells us v equal to 1.

00:57:41.050 --> 00:57:43.060
And now we'll just
calculate the entropy.

00:57:43.060 --> 00:57:44.950
The average of these
quantities which

00:57:44.950 --> 00:57:52.767
is Pu equal to 0 times Hv given
u equal to 0 plus Pu equal to 1

00:57:52.767 --> 00:57:55.220
Hv u equal to 1.

00:57:55.220 --> 00:58:00.830
And then you can do 1/2
times 1 plus 1/2 times 0.

00:58:00.830 --> 00:58:04.940
Because we saw that u is only
1/2 here, so this is just half.

00:58:08.730 --> 00:58:12.540
And a few things are computed at
home was that the entropy of u

00:58:12.540 --> 00:58:19.330
is 1 because its Bernoulli
half, entropy of v is 0.811.

00:58:19.330 --> 00:58:22.210
So you already see that
the entropy of this guy

00:58:22.210 --> 00:58:25.720
is bigger than or equal to this.

00:58:25.720 --> 00:58:29.140
So you see that knowing u has
decreased your uncertainty

00:58:29.140 --> 00:58:32.100
about v.

00:58:32.100 --> 00:58:36.240
So v on its own had
some uncertainty, 0.811,

00:58:36.240 --> 00:58:39.610
but knowing u
reduced it to half.

00:58:39.610 --> 00:58:43.060
But you see that if you know
u equal to 0, that actually

00:58:43.060 --> 00:58:49.030
increases your uncertainty,
because knowing u equal to 1

00:58:49.030 --> 00:58:52.030
reduces your uncertainty
about v, knowing u equal to 0

00:58:52.030 --> 00:58:55.420
increases your uncertainty
about v, and then on average,

00:58:55.420 --> 00:58:57.190
it still gets lower.

00:58:57.190 --> 00:59:00.040
And this is a
calculation that you

00:59:00.040 --> 00:59:03.490
can do very easily,
which is you just take

00:59:03.490 --> 00:59:07.855
all four things in the table
and do this sort of thing.

00:59:18.890 --> 00:59:25.020
So 1/2 plus 1/2.

00:59:25.020 --> 00:59:27.800
I think it will be 3/2.

00:59:27.800 --> 00:59:31.745
And you can verify that this is
actually equal to this thing--

00:59:31.745 --> 00:59:34.490
Hu plus Hv given u.

00:59:34.490 --> 00:59:40.030
So this is the chain
rule for entropies.

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:41.980
So the joint entropy
of u and v is actually

00:59:41.980 --> 00:59:44.860
equal to the entropy of u
plus the entropy of v given u.

00:59:44.860 --> 00:59:46.780
So the overall
uncertainty in u and v

00:59:46.780 --> 00:59:49.390
combined is the
uncertainty v in u

00:59:49.390 --> 00:59:51.565
and the uncertainty
in v given u.

00:59:54.170 --> 01:00:00.710
So a few properties about
conditional entropy.

01:00:00.710 --> 01:00:02.990
First is that conditioning
reduces entropy,

01:00:02.990 --> 01:00:05.390
which is that
entropy of u given v

01:00:05.390 --> 01:00:08.060
will be less than or
equal to u with equality

01:00:08.060 --> 01:00:10.010
if and if u and v
are independent.

01:00:10.010 --> 01:00:13.310
So if u and v are independent
they will have the same.

01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:18.930
So knowing anything about v
will tell you nothing about u.

01:00:18.930 --> 01:00:23.060
And this is the thing I was
telling you earlier about like,

01:00:23.060 --> 01:00:25.670
a specific piece of evidence
can increase your uncertainty

01:00:25.670 --> 01:00:29.720
but on average, knowledge will
always reduce your uncertainty.

01:00:29.720 --> 01:00:31.670
Knowing things is
good on average.

01:00:34.910 --> 01:00:37.430
Let me just show all
the properties at once.

01:00:37.430 --> 01:00:41.266
Then we have the chain rule
of entropies, which is this.

01:00:41.266 --> 01:00:43.560
At the entropy
joint entropy is--

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:48.030
if you remember the chain rule
we saw before for iid if you

01:00:48.030 --> 01:00:54.850
recall, not iid just
for independent,

01:00:54.850 --> 01:00:58.330
the entropy was some
of these entropies.

01:00:58.330 --> 01:01:00.560
But for non-iid in
a more general case,

01:01:00.560 --> 01:01:02.260
it is actually this formula--

01:01:02.260 --> 01:01:06.550
HU plus HV given U.

01:01:06.550 --> 01:01:08.860
Let me quickly state it
in terms of compression

01:01:08.860 --> 01:01:10.870
because that's the
class we are in.

01:01:10.870 --> 01:01:14.650
So you remember HU was
the best thing you can do

01:01:14.650 --> 01:01:16.960
for compressing U on its own.

01:01:16.960 --> 01:01:18.520
HV was the best
thing you could do

01:01:18.520 --> 01:01:20.620
for compressing V on its own.

01:01:20.620 --> 01:01:23.530
And in fact, HV
given U is the best

01:01:23.530 --> 01:01:27.540
thing you can do for
compressing V if you know U.

01:01:27.540 --> 01:01:30.240
So this is the minimum number of
bits you can use to compress V

01:01:30.240 --> 01:01:34.210
if you knew U. So
what we are saying

01:01:34.210 --> 01:01:36.610
is if you want to
jointly compress U and V,

01:01:36.610 --> 01:01:39.250
what you could do is you
first compress U on its own

01:01:39.250 --> 01:01:42.820
and then you compress V
given that you know U.

01:01:42.820 --> 01:01:45.100
So it's a two-step compression
process where you first

01:01:45.100 --> 01:01:46.642
compress just U and
then you compress

01:01:46.642 --> 01:01:50.090
V given the knowledge of U.

01:01:50.090 --> 01:01:51.880
And the bits add up basically.

01:01:51.880 --> 01:01:55.930
So maybe this guy uses one bit
and this guy uses like 1/2 bit.

01:01:55.930 --> 01:01:58.540
And so overall,
you get 3/2 2 bits

01:01:58.540 --> 01:02:00.730
for jointly compressing U and V.

01:02:00.730 --> 01:02:03.190
And the last thing
which you see here

01:02:03.190 --> 01:02:06.400
is saying that the joint
entropy between U and V

01:02:06.400 --> 01:02:19.330
is either equal to or smaller
than the sum of the entropies,

01:02:19.330 --> 01:02:21.940
this means that if
you independently

01:02:21.940 --> 01:02:25.210
compress U and V. So let's
take an extreme example.

01:02:29.230 --> 01:02:32.680
Let's say you had a random
variable which was either

01:02:32.680 --> 01:02:35.140
1 or 0, and then you had
a second random variable

01:02:35.140 --> 01:02:38.050
which was always the opposite
of the first random variable.

01:02:38.050 --> 01:02:40.870
So if the first random variable
is 1, then the other guy is 0.

01:02:40.870 --> 01:02:43.120
If the first one is
0, the other is 1.

01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:45.700
So if you compress
them independently

01:02:45.700 --> 01:02:48.790
they will take 1 bit
each, whereas if you

01:02:48.790 --> 01:02:51.820
use the knowledge of the first
guy to compress the other guy,

01:02:51.820 --> 01:02:54.970
then you can compress
the second one for free.

01:02:54.970 --> 01:02:58.750
So I hope this is somewhat--

01:02:58.750 --> 01:03:04.030
so if we do this
simple example where

01:03:04.030 --> 01:03:11.560
if U is Bernoulli half
and V is 1 minus U,

01:03:11.560 --> 01:03:15.700
so HU will be 1
and so will be HV,

01:03:15.700 --> 01:03:19.460
but the joint entropy of U
and V is actually still 1.

01:03:19.460 --> 01:03:25.410
So this is less than HU plus HV.

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:27.720
Here knowing one of
them basically tells you

01:03:27.720 --> 01:03:28.650
the other one.

01:03:28.650 --> 01:03:31.170
So compressing them
jointly is much better

01:03:31.170 --> 01:03:32.910
than compressing
them independently.

01:03:32.910 --> 01:03:36.870
So that is the whole
idea about all of this.

01:03:36.870 --> 01:03:40.290
There is a question
in the quiz, there

01:03:40.290 --> 01:03:41.850
is a question in the homework.

01:03:41.850 --> 01:03:45.030
So do those and I hope you
will get very comfortable

01:03:45.030 --> 01:03:46.350
with these quantities.

01:03:49.420 --> 01:03:51.890
Any questions?

01:03:51.890 --> 01:03:53.870
Just in general you
can have this condition

01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:55.490
on the past n things
instead of just

01:03:55.490 --> 01:03:59.020
conditioning on the past thing.

01:03:59.020 --> 01:04:00.270
Any questions?

01:04:08.840 --> 01:04:09.920
OK.

01:04:09.920 --> 01:04:15.320
Now we define the real
quantity for stationary sources

01:04:15.320 --> 01:04:20.305
which is that what we want to do
is we want to-- we had entropy.

01:04:20.305 --> 01:04:21.930
Entropy was the best
thing you could do

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:25.950
for compressing iid sequences.

01:04:25.950 --> 01:04:29.190
Now we want to compress
stationary sequences or Markov

01:04:29.190 --> 01:04:30.540
or any of these guys.

01:04:30.540 --> 01:04:35.520
So we will define something
called entropy rate which

01:04:35.520 --> 01:04:42.850
is defined for any stationary
process and not only one

01:04:42.850 --> 01:04:44.470
but two ways of defining it.

01:04:44.470 --> 01:04:48.760
So sort of buy one get
one free sort of deal.

01:04:51.910 --> 01:04:54.100
There are these two definitions.

01:04:54.100 --> 01:04:57.160
We won't go into the proofs, we
won't go into very specifics,

01:04:57.160 --> 01:05:01.020
but the basic idea
is that entropy rate

01:05:01.020 --> 01:05:04.320
of a stationary process
is defined in two ways.

01:05:04.320 --> 01:05:09.800
The first way is you take
the past and symbols.

01:05:09.800 --> 01:05:11.285
You compute up.

01:05:11.285 --> 01:05:14.128
If you know the past, how
much is left in the future?

01:05:14.128 --> 01:05:15.920
Basically, that's what
you're trying to do.

01:05:15.920 --> 01:05:17.810
So if you knew the
first million symbols,

01:05:17.810 --> 01:05:20.630
what is the remaining
entropy in the next symbol?

01:05:20.630 --> 01:05:23.090
How much is each
next symbol adding?

01:05:23.090 --> 01:05:24.530
Basically, the uncertainty.

01:05:24.530 --> 01:05:27.990
That is your entropy rate
and it's defined like this.

01:05:27.990 --> 01:05:29.210
This is the limit.

01:05:29.210 --> 01:05:31.795
The second way to
define it is, again, you

01:05:31.795 --> 01:05:33.170
can think in terms
of compression

01:05:33.170 --> 01:05:37.150
if I want to jointly
compress n symbols at a time

01:05:37.150 --> 01:05:39.760
and then take the per
symbol how much am

01:05:39.760 --> 01:05:41.923
I paying for compression.

01:05:41.923 --> 01:05:43.090
And then you take the limit.

01:05:43.090 --> 01:05:45.215
So you make the blocks
bigger and bigger and bigger

01:05:45.215 --> 01:05:47.380
and bigger, and you see
per symbol how much do

01:05:47.380 --> 01:05:49.360
I need to pay for compression.

01:05:49.360 --> 01:05:51.100
So there are two
ways of defining it.

01:05:51.100 --> 01:05:53.800
And in fact, you can
show that these two are

01:05:53.800 --> 01:05:58.180
equal for any stationary
process and the limit is

01:05:58.180 --> 01:06:01.878
called the entropy rate.

01:06:01.878 --> 01:06:03.670
You won't have that
much use for it really.

01:06:03.670 --> 01:06:05.980
I think if you want to remember
one of these definitions,

01:06:05.980 --> 01:06:06.790
remember this guy.

01:06:06.790 --> 01:06:07.665
This is the main one.

01:06:07.665 --> 01:06:11.580
This is the one we'll
use more frequently.

01:06:11.580 --> 01:06:14.710
This is in terms of
like how much extra--

01:06:14.710 --> 01:06:16.410
let me write it down.

01:06:16.410 --> 01:06:32.450
So this is on average bits
per symbol for large blocks.

01:06:32.450 --> 01:06:46.040
And this one is additional
entropy first symbol

01:06:46.040 --> 01:06:47.330
given the past.

01:06:47.330 --> 01:06:49.538
So each symbol, how
much extra entropy

01:06:49.538 --> 01:06:51.080
is it adding to your
thing, and we'll

01:06:51.080 --> 01:06:52.788
look at a couple of
examples in a minute.

01:06:56.797 --> 01:06:58.255
I guess we'll look
at the examples.

01:07:01.800 --> 01:07:04.520
So actually what happens
is for a fair coin toss

01:07:04.520 --> 01:07:10.570
if you look at this guy U1
through Un divided by n,

01:07:10.570 --> 01:07:14.140
if you remember from
your previous lectures

01:07:14.140 --> 01:07:16.420
this is just this.

01:07:16.420 --> 01:07:19.290
So this is just 1 bit.

01:07:19.290 --> 01:07:21.570
Basically, the joint entropy
of the first and symbols

01:07:21.570 --> 01:07:24.570
is just n times the entropy
of the first symbol,

01:07:24.570 --> 01:07:25.350
which is 1 bit.

01:07:25.350 --> 01:07:30.000
So for iid sequences in
general, the entropy rate

01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:32.010
is just equal to the entropy.

01:07:32.010 --> 01:07:38.720
So for iid, entropy rate
is equal to entropy.

01:07:43.630 --> 01:07:46.750
The Markov thing
is a bit trickier.

01:07:46.750 --> 01:07:49.690
Maybe let's just try
to compute this one.

01:07:53.600 --> 01:07:56.210
So let me go through the logic.

01:07:56.210 --> 01:07:58.740
So the idea is--

01:07:58.740 --> 01:08:03.590
so if you know u1, so
you can assume let's

01:08:03.590 --> 01:08:07.280
say u1 is equal
to 0, for example.

01:08:07.280 --> 01:08:14.890
u2 takes two values
with equal probability.

01:08:14.890 --> 01:08:19.220
We just saw this example
earlier in class.

01:08:19.220 --> 01:08:22.700
We saw that if u1 is 0
then u2 can be either 0

01:08:22.700 --> 01:08:26.930
or it can be 1 each with
half of probability.

01:08:26.930 --> 01:08:30.590
So therefore, it's easy
to convince yourself

01:08:30.590 --> 01:08:35.810
that actually Hu2 given
u1 is equal to the entropy

01:08:35.810 --> 01:08:37.580
of a Bernoulli half
random variable

01:08:37.580 --> 01:08:40.340
because if you take two
values with equal probability

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:43.800
and this is just one bit.

01:08:43.800 --> 01:08:45.689
And then what you
can convince yourself

01:08:45.689 --> 01:08:48.465
is that Un given Un
minus 1 dot, dot,

01:08:48.465 --> 01:08:53.609
dot u1 is actually just
equal to Un given Un minus 1.

01:08:53.609 --> 01:08:54.689
And why is this?

01:08:54.689 --> 01:08:56.520
This is because of
the Markov property.

01:08:56.520 --> 01:08:59.279
Markov property says
that if the last symbol

01:08:59.279 --> 01:09:00.899
the previous symbols
have got nothing

01:09:00.899 --> 01:09:02.550
to do with the next symbol.

01:09:02.550 --> 01:09:04.724
Just that last symbol is
the one you care about.

01:09:09.970 --> 01:09:11.050
So that's it.

01:09:11.050 --> 01:09:13.000
Basically, you get the
entropy rate is 1 bit.

01:09:17.160 --> 01:09:20.640
Let me pause here for a second.

01:09:20.640 --> 01:09:23.490
There is a question
very similar,

01:09:23.490 --> 01:09:26.279
feel free to compute
this carefully.

01:09:37.270 --> 01:09:39.250
Let me just write it clearly.

01:09:39.250 --> 01:09:45.620
So for iid, there is an entropy
rate written in different ways

01:09:45.620 --> 01:09:47.359
depending on the text.

01:09:47.359 --> 01:09:50.660
I will just try to use a
boldface italics type U

01:09:50.660 --> 01:09:52.745
is equal to the Hu1 basically.

01:09:52.745 --> 01:09:54.740
For iid, the
entropy rate is just

01:09:54.740 --> 01:09:57.800
the entropy of each symbol.

01:09:57.800 --> 01:10:07.910
For kth order Markov in
general, the entropy rate

01:10:07.910 --> 01:10:12.320
is the entropy of the k
plus 1 at the symbol given

01:10:12.320 --> 01:10:17.040
the first case symbols.

01:10:17.040 --> 01:10:19.860
So in these cases, the entropy
rate is easy to compute.

01:10:19.860 --> 01:10:22.820
And basically, the
idea is that if you

01:10:22.820 --> 01:10:25.470
know the past case symbols,
how much uncertainty

01:10:25.470 --> 01:10:27.140
is in the next symbol?

01:10:27.140 --> 01:10:30.920
That's the whole idea here.

01:10:30.920 --> 01:10:33.830
Let's look at a real example.

01:10:33.830 --> 01:10:35.600
English.

01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:38.720
So you can take a lot of
English texts like a big corpus.

01:10:38.720 --> 01:10:41.270
And then what you
can try to do is try

01:10:41.270 --> 01:10:44.630
to estimate the probabilities--

01:10:44.630 --> 01:10:46.940
you estimate the
probability distribution.

01:10:46.940 --> 01:10:49.070
You make a probability
model out of English.

01:10:49.070 --> 01:10:51.200
You take two symbols
at a time, you

01:10:51.200 --> 01:10:54.038
take three symbols at a time,
three characters at a time,

01:10:54.038 --> 01:10:55.580
four characters at
a time, and so on,

01:10:55.580 --> 01:10:57.290
and you make this
probability table.

01:10:57.290 --> 01:11:05.630
So what you could do is you
could find probability of u

01:11:05.630 --> 01:11:07.670
or you can find
probability of u1,

01:11:07.670 --> 01:11:09.500
u2 like two
characters at a time,

01:11:09.500 --> 01:11:13.380
or you do u1, u2, u3, and so on.

01:11:13.380 --> 01:11:17.600
So you can make more and more
complicated models of English.

01:11:17.600 --> 01:11:19.880
So for example, how
would you estimate this?

01:11:19.880 --> 01:11:23.990
So if you do what is the
probability that u1 is A and u2

01:11:23.990 --> 01:11:28.490
is C, so this will
be number of times

01:11:28.490 --> 01:11:33.650
you have seen x some xn
equal to A and xn plus 1

01:11:33.650 --> 01:11:38.760
equal to C divided by
total sort of thing.

01:11:38.760 --> 01:11:41.220
So you just
empirically estimate.

01:11:41.220 --> 01:11:43.830
If you have seen this AC
combination a million times

01:11:43.830 --> 01:11:47.040
out of a billion times, then you
do like million by billion sort

01:11:47.040 --> 01:11:49.440
of thing.

01:11:49.440 --> 01:11:52.800
So somebody did this
for some English corpus.

01:11:52.800 --> 01:11:55.110
And what they found was
if you assume it's iid,

01:11:55.110 --> 01:11:57.960
so zeroth order
Markov chain, they

01:11:57.960 --> 01:12:00.420
got 4.7 six bits per letter.

01:12:00.420 --> 01:12:04.250
So this is what they got.

01:12:04.250 --> 01:12:07.760
And as you might imagine, as
you take more and more history,

01:12:07.760 --> 01:12:10.010
your uncertainty will
reduce more and more

01:12:10.010 --> 01:12:12.410
because you can use that
to predict the next symbol.

01:12:12.410 --> 01:12:14.360
So if you take a
first-order Markov chain,

01:12:14.360 --> 01:12:16.452
so if you use the
previous symbol

01:12:16.452 --> 01:12:18.410
to predict the next
symbol, so now suddenly you

01:12:18.410 --> 01:12:20.390
can use the fact
that after a Q you

01:12:20.390 --> 01:12:23.510
mostly get a U sort of
thing, and suddenly you

01:12:23.510 --> 01:12:24.740
do slightly better.

01:12:24.740 --> 01:12:27.980
You do 4.76 to 4.03.

01:12:27.980 --> 01:12:33.340
So your conditional
entropy reduces.

01:12:33.340 --> 01:12:35.980
Maybe you take four
previous characters.

01:12:35.980 --> 01:12:39.820
For example, if you
see T, H, I, then

01:12:39.820 --> 01:12:43.510
it's maybe it's a this
or it's a thing, so n.

01:12:43.510 --> 01:12:46.750
So you are even more certain
about your prediction.

01:12:46.750 --> 01:12:49.240
And the entropy reduces further.

01:12:49.240 --> 01:12:51.410
It becomes 2.8.

01:12:51.410 --> 01:12:55.640
And then Shannon in 1952
actually did a very interesting

01:12:55.640 --> 01:12:59.680
experiment where he
got a few people.

01:12:59.680 --> 01:13:00.850
I think his wife, actually.

01:13:00.850 --> 01:13:03.880
But he got some test
subjects, basically.

01:13:03.880 --> 01:13:08.830
And they gave them some
symbols in some English text

01:13:08.830 --> 01:13:11.110
and asked them to
predict the next symbol.

01:13:11.110 --> 01:13:13.630
And then they predicted the
next symbol and they say

01:13:13.630 --> 01:13:15.370
what is the probability of--

01:13:15.370 --> 01:13:17.410
did they predict the
right one or not?

01:13:17.410 --> 01:13:19.450
And then you predict
the next symbol.

01:13:19.450 --> 01:13:22.360
They got a human to actually
predict each symbol.

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:25.510
And they use that to estimate
the entropy in English,

01:13:25.510 --> 01:13:27.400
how much uncertainty
is in English,

01:13:27.400 --> 01:13:29.920
assuming that a human
is a perfect predictor.

01:13:29.920 --> 01:13:33.690
And they got 1.3.

01:13:33.690 --> 01:13:35.217
You see a fourth--
it's much better

01:13:35.217 --> 01:13:36.300
than a fourth-order model.

01:13:36.300 --> 01:13:38.040
And which is true.

01:13:38.040 --> 01:13:40.527
The previous word tells you
a lot about the next word,

01:13:40.527 --> 01:13:43.110
the previous sentence tells you
a lot about the next sentence.

01:13:43.110 --> 01:13:46.440
So the dependence goes a
bit further than just four

01:13:46.440 --> 01:13:49.540
characters.

01:13:49.540 --> 01:13:52.990
And obviously, now LLMs
have beaten humans.

01:13:52.990 --> 01:13:56.320
So if you look at
the latest models

01:13:56.320 --> 01:13:58.000
we'll look at them
in the next lecture,

01:13:58.000 --> 01:14:01.600
they get a rate which
is even lower than this.

01:14:01.600 --> 01:14:03.730
So they have become
even stronger predictors

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:08.260
of English than
humans at this point,

01:14:08.260 --> 01:14:12.040
subject to the experiment
being done in a specific way.

01:14:15.190 --> 01:14:16.180
OK.

01:14:16.180 --> 01:14:17.485
So last slide for today.

01:14:21.810 --> 01:14:25.290
Just in the previous one you
saw we said bits per letter

01:14:25.290 --> 01:14:28.780
and I've been using compression
analogies all the time.

01:14:28.780 --> 01:14:40.060
So basically kth order Markov
source, the best compression

01:14:40.060 --> 01:14:41.620
you can do is basically this--

01:14:41.620 --> 01:14:45.670
the Huk given Uk minus
1 dot, dot, dot U1.

01:14:45.670 --> 01:14:50.250
And in general for
a stationary source,

01:14:50.250 --> 01:14:51.780
entropy rate is the
best compression

01:14:51.780 --> 01:14:52.890
you can hope to achieve.

01:14:52.890 --> 01:14:54.060
This is the takeaway.

01:14:54.060 --> 01:14:57.060
So just like the AEP
property or typical sets

01:14:57.060 --> 01:14:59.640
that you read
about in lecture 5,

01:14:59.640 --> 01:15:02.340
there is a similar
property called

01:15:02.340 --> 01:15:04.980
Shannon-McMillan-Breiman
theorem, which

01:15:04.980 --> 01:15:08.130
says that the probabilities
of these blocks

01:15:08.130 --> 01:15:11.188
are roughly equal to 2
power minus entropy rate.

01:15:11.188 --> 01:15:13.230
So it's exactly the same
property you saw before.

01:15:13.230 --> 01:15:15.773
Just replace entropy
by entropy rate.

01:15:15.773 --> 01:15:17.190
You need some
technical conditions

01:15:17.190 --> 01:15:22.900
which we won't go into,
but basically, that's

01:15:22.900 --> 01:15:25.210
why these quantities are
important because, again, they

01:15:25.210 --> 01:15:27.820
give us the best you can
do in terms of compression.

01:15:27.820 --> 01:15:30.610
If you have a first-order
Markov source then Hu2 given Un

01:15:30.610 --> 01:15:32.110
is the best compression
you can get.

01:15:32.110 --> 01:15:34.330
And if you have a general
stationary source,

01:15:34.330 --> 01:15:38.875
the best compression you
can get is the entropy rate.

01:15:42.730 --> 01:15:45.310
So in the next lecture what
